Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream iss(markdown);

    while (std::getline(iss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a first paragraph\nThis is a second paragraph\nThis is a third paragraph";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and wraps each line in a `<p>` tag to create an HTML paragraph. The resulting HTML string is returned.

Note that this function assumes that the markdown text is formatted in a simple way, with each line representing a paragraph. If the markdown text is more complex, you may need to add additional logic to handle it.

